Lab 15: Integrated IdM Back-End Configuration
Objectives
Integrate Red Hat Identity Management (IdM) or LDAP as an authentication backend for Keystone
Synchronize users, groups, and roles between IdM/LDAP and Keystone
Implement password policies and user lifecycle management
Prerequisites
Red Hat Enterprise Linux 8/9 or CentOS Stream 8/9 system
Administrative access to the system (root or sudo privileges)
Red Hat Identity Management (IdM) server already configured
OpenStack environment with Keystone installed
Basic understanding of LDAP concepts
Network connectivity between Keystone and IdM servers
Lab Setup
Before beginning, ensure the following packages are installed:

sudo dnf install -y openstack-keystone python3-ldappool python3-ldap
Task 1: Configure Keystone for IdM/LDAP Integration
Subtask 1.1: Configure LDAP Backend in Keystone
Edit the Keystone configuration file:
sudo vi /etc/keystone/keystone.conf
Add or modify the following sections:
[identity]
driver = ldap

[ldap]
url = ldap://idm.example.com
user = uid=admin,cn=users,cn=accounts,dc=example,dc=com
password = IdMAdminPassword
suffix = cn=accounts,dc=example,dc=com
user_tree_dn = cn=users,cn=accounts,dc=example,dc=com
user_objectclass = person
user_id_attribute = uid
user_name_attribute = uid
user_mail_attribute = mail
user_pass_attribute = userPassword
group_tree_dn = cn=groups,cn=accounts,dc=example,dc=com
group_objectclass = groupOfNames
group_id_attribute = cn
group_name_attribute = cn
group_member_attribute = member
user_allow_create = False
user_allow_update = False
user_allow_delete = False
group_allow_create = False
group_allow_update = False
group_allow_delete = False
Restart the Keystone service:
sudo systemctl restart openstack-keystone
Expected Outcome: Keystone should now be able to communicate with the IdM server using LDAP.

Troubleshooting Tip: If connection fails, verify:

LDAP URL is correct
Firewall allows LDAP traffic (port 389)
Credentials are correct
TLS certificates are trusted if using LDAPS
Task 2: Synchronize Users, Groups, and Roles
Subtask 2.1: Map LDAP Groups to Keystone Roles
Create a mapping file for roles:
sudo vi /etc/keystone/domains/keystone.idm.conf
Add role mapping configuration:
[identity]
driver = ldap

[ldap]
# Previous LDAP configuration remains

[role_mapping]
admin = admins
member = users
reader = guests
Apply the configuration:
sudo keystone-manage mapping_populate --domain idm
Expected Outcome: LDAP groups should now be mapped to corresponding Keystone roles.

Subtask 2.2: Verify Synchronization
List users from IdM in Keystone:
openstack user list --domain idm
List groups and their role assignments:
openstack role assignment list --names
Expected Outcome: You should see users and groups from IdM appearing in Keystone with proper role assignments.

Task 3: Implement Password Policies and User Lifecycle Management
Subtask 3.1: Configure Password Policies in IdM
On the IdM server, set password policy:
sudo ipa pwpolicy-mod --maxlife=90 --minlife=1 --history=5 --minclasses=4 --minlength=12 --maxfail=3 --failinterval=5 --lockouttime=300
Expected Outcome: Password policy will enforce:

90-day maximum password age
12-character minimum length
4 character classes required
3 failed attempts lock account for 5 minutes
Subtask 3.2: Configure Keystone for Password Policy Enforcement
Edit Keystone configuration:
sudo vi /etc/keystone/policy.json
Add password policy enforcement rules:
{
    "identity:change_password": "rule:admin_required or user_id:%(target.user.id)s",
    "identity:enforce_password_policy": "rule:admin_required"
}
Restart Keystone:
sudo systemctl restart openstack-keystone
Subtask 3.3: Test User Lifecycle
Create a test user in IdM:
sudo ipa user-add testuser --first=Test --last=User --password
Verify the user appears in Keystone:
openstack user show testuser --domain idm
Disable the user in IdM:
sudo ipa user-disable testuser
Verify access is blocked in Keystone:
openstack token issue --os-username testuser --os-password Password123
Expected Outcome: The disabled user should not be able to authenticate with Keystone.

Conclusion
In this lab, you have successfully:

Integrated Red Hat Identity Management (IdM) as an external authentication backend for Keystone
Synchronized users, groups, and roles between IdM and Keystone
Implemented password policies and user lifecycle management
The integration allows OpenStack to leverage existing enterprise identity management systems, providing:

Centralized user management
Consistent security policies
Simplified user experience
Better compliance with organizational security requirements
